WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.320
Alright.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.200
Welcome back.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:18.400
So, the next four lectures are also oriented
towards discussion of primarily clinical theories

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.989
of psychology and psychopathology.

00:00:23.989 --> 00:00:32.390
We’re going to talk about lines of thinking
that, in some ways, run parallel to those

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:38.540
of the psychoanalysts because the thinkers
we’re going to discuss now, with a few small

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.070
exceptions—well, all of them—all of the
thinkers we’re going to discuss in the next

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:50.480
four lectures were either influenced heavily
by Freud, Jung, and Adler or were themselves

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.610
thinkers who influenced Freud and Jung.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:01:02.989
So, there’s an intellectual continuity stretching
over—with people who are deeply educated

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:08.100
and who very thoughtful there’s intellectually
continuity of ideas stretching over exceptional

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.009
spans of time.

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:16.909
And so that sort of relationship is exactly
what you would imagine.

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:22.600
Rogers had a variety of influences—so let’s
say Freud and Jung and Adler—but he was

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.799
also very much influenced by phenomenological
ideas.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:38.020
And the phenomenologists were, and are—phenomenology
is a brand of philosophy, which I’ll explain

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:44.520
more deeply, and the other primary domain
of influence on Rogers was Christianity likely

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.389
and then Christianity also via probably Northern
European culture as far as I can tell.

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:55.810
The reason I’m talking specifically about
Christianity in relationship to Rogers is

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.060
because as a young man he was a fundamentalist
Christian and he was a seminarian.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:08.259
So he was quite oriented towards mainline
Protestant Christianity, but when he was in

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:12.660
university he had a crisis of belief I suppose.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.210
He was even going to go to China as a missionary
at one point.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:21.620
But he had a crisis of belief in university
because he had also been scientifically trained.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:28.280
His father was a farmer who used rather advanced
farming techniques, and so Rogers was trained

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.480
to think scientifically from a very young
age.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.080
And he experienced the conflict between his
religious belief and his scientific presuppositions

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:43.110
quite intensely, and in that battle his formal
Christian belief was the loser.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:49.610
However, his thinking is very much influenced
by fundamentalist Protestant presuppositions

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:59.450
and the effect of those I’ll describe as
we walk through Rogers’ thinking.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:08.360
Now, it’s important to understand the philosophical
underpinnings of a global personality or psychology

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:19.770
theory because every theory is set within
a set of assumptions—things that you have

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.790
to assume to be true in order to proceed with
a theory.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:27.030
They’re like the axioms in classical geometry.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.350
You can’t prove the axioms; you have to
accept them.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:33.050
Once you accept the axioms then you can move
forward and use the whole system.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:39.410
I think the reason that systems have to have
axioms, which are elements of belief, is because

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.130
every system is incomplete, right?

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.350
It doesn’t cover the entire world.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:49.930
And so, in some sense, you need a place to
dispense with your ignorance and the way you

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.600
do that is by making assumptions.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.100
We’re going to act as if the following is
true.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.030
And then you can proceed with a partial theory
and you’ll have some success with it.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:06.250
But it’s useful to understand the presuppositions
because it orients you properly towards the

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.250
theory.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:14.150
Now Rogers is a phenomenologist, and phenomenology
is a brand of philosophy, which I believe

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:22.840
started with Edmund Husserl but was probably
made most influential by his student Martin

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.340
Heidegger.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:34.490
Now Heidegger was a philosopher I suppose
somewhat like Nietzsche in that his thinking

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:40.440
ran very much counter to what you might describe
as general academic philosophy.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:47.030
For example, Heidegger believed that ever
since the time of Plato, which is when people

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:54.460
think philosophy began more or less, Western
philosophy had taken an erroneous direction:

00:04:54.460 --> 00:05:00.610
it had become too concerned with the nature
of structures of thought and not concerned

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.530
enough about the nature of being.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.570
And so you can determine those two things.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:10.090
Being is how things are and thought, of course,
is how you represent things.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:22.220
And one of Heidegger’s missions was to reestablish
being as the central reality of philosophy.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:28.880
Now that begs a question, which is: “What
is being?”

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:36.200
Well, to conceptualize reality as being is
not to conceptualize it the same way that

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:47.620
you conceptualize it in terms of modern Western
thought, because one of the presuppositions

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:54.460
at the basis of modern Western thought is
that the most intelligent way to divide up

00:05:54.460 --> 00:06:02.570
the world is in terms of subjective and objective
experience or subjective experience and objective

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.570
world.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:10.260
So the fundamental scientific presupposition,
you might say, is that there is an objective

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.160
world; it’s the real thing.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:18.930
You come to that by performing certain procedures,
documenting them, describing the outcome of

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:24.180
those procedures, documenting them, and then
allowing other people to do the same thing

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:29.419
operating under the presupposition that if
they experience the same outcome from the

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:31.670
procedures, then what they’re experiencing
is real.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.139
That’s basically the scientific method,
right?

00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:35.139
Method.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:36.139
Results.

00:06:36.139 --> 00:06:38.710
You detail the method, which is how you act
on the world; you get the results.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:44.180
You compare them, maybe across multiple observations
that you do; and then other people do multiple

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:49.350
observations; you come up with a consensus,
but it’s a method bound consensus.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:53.650
And then of course you abstract out general
principles from that and it works very powerfully,

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:54.650
right?

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.000
Make no mistake about it.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.840
The idea that there is an objective external
world and that that’s the best way to conceptualize

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.260
reality is an unbelievably powerful tool.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:12.770
Now, whether or not it’s an accurate description
of reality: that’s a much more difficult

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:13.870
question.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:18.460
And it’s partly a matter of definition,
actually.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:22.480
You know, it’s the case there are different
forms of geometry; you might know this.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:27.620
You can start from the presupposition that
reality has a three-dimensional structure—and

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:32.440
then you’re basically within the realm of
Euclidean geometry—but you can also start

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.410
with the presupposition that reality has a
four-dimensional structure.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.880
And that kind of geometry was invented in
the late 1800’s and it turned out to be

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.639
the perfect geometry to describe the universe
that Einstein conceptualized when he came

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:49.660
up with his great theories in the second decade,
thereabouts, of the twentieth century.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.520
So it’s interesting because can start from
different assumptions, build tools on the

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.080
basis of those assumptions, and sometimes
you find out that those tools work very well

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.570
indeed.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:10.550
Now, it’s not that easy to think of flaws
in the subjective experience/ objective world

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:16.110
conceptualization, and that’s partly—for
us as modern people it forms the basis of

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.550
so much of what we think that we just think
about it as axiomatic.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:20.850
It’s just true.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.830
It doesn’t bear any question.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:28.979
But I’m going to offer you a couple examples
of why it might bear some questioning and

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:33.149
you can think about it.

00:08:33.149 --> 00:08:39.440
The first problem is is that the classical
scientific view of the world has a very difficult

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.110
time accounting for certain things.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:48.529
So, for example—these are called “qualia”
often by people who are interested in consciousness—knowing

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:52.240
the wavelength of light doesn’t seem to
tell you very much about the subjective experience

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:53.800
of color.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:58.620
You know, we experience color, for example,
in a non-continuous fashion, right, because

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.100
we see discrete colors like you’d see in
a rainbow.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.690
I mean they shade into each other to some
degree but it’s not like they go from light

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:06.510
red to dark red in a continuum.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.950
There are clear demarcations of color.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:12.950
Well, that’s because of the way your brain
is wired.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:17.760
You know, it seems like, over the course of
evolution, what we’ve learned is that parsing

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:21.990
up that wavelength fragment in terms of categories,
which would be the different colors that we

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:24.990
see, has substantial practical utility.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:29.140
Now one of the things you might ask is, “Well,
do those colors exist?”

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:35.490
Well, objectively they don’t seem to exist
as colors, partly because the wavelengths

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:37.230
that we see are actually continuous.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:43.630
So when one color shades into another, there’s
no radical transformation in wavelength.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:45.890
It’s a continuum but we see it categorically.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:47.149
Okay, so is that real or not.

00:09:47.149 --> 00:09:49.430
Are the colors we see real or not?

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:53.510
Well, that’s a good question.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:57.370
You can’t get a collective experience of
pain.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:01.620
You can get a collective experience of the
blackboard, but pain seems to be something

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.420
that’s limited to subjective perception.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.730
But it’s very difficult—no one acts as
if pain isn’t real.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:16.990
I mean I would say that of all the things
that you might consider real we act as if

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:18.820
pain is the most real thing.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:22.120
You certainly can’t argue out of it for
example.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.860
It seems like a fundamental element of experience.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:27.340
Okay, so that’s two places.

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:36.080
We also have no account in the subjective/
objective breakup bifurcation of the world—we

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.560
have no explanation whatsoever for consciousness.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.930
And that seems like a big problem, right,
because you can make a case that there is

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.610
no being without consciousness.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.620
Like, “Is there something if there’s no
one there to perceive it?”

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.740
By the way, that is not the same question
as, “If a tree falls in the forest and there’s

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.470
no one there to hear it, does it make a sound?”

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.020
It’s not the same question.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:01.339
It sounds superficially the same, but this
is a much more complex question because we

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:07.899
don’t know, for example, if—we don’t
know the relationship between time and consciousness.

00:11:07.899 --> 00:11:10.800
We have a certain perception of time.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.290
We have a certain temporal realm in which
we exist.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:18.790
Our refresh rate for our vision, for example,
is about sixty hertz, which is why you can

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:22.620
see computer screens flicker if they have
a refresh rate slower than that.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:28.600
So sixty hertz to us looks continuous but,
you know, there are a lot of microseconds

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.260
in a second and for a computer—especially
a fast-processing computer that can do maybe

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:39.240
a trillion operations a second—if it was
watching, so to speak, a movie it would just

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:44.580
see a still picture that was there forever
and a another still picture that was there

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:45.580
forever.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.770
And it wouldn’t have the impression of continuous
movement.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:53.690
So there’s this weird subjective element
to time that’s very difficult to understand.

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:59.490
And then here’s a more complex criticism
of the subject/ object idea.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.870
I thought about this for a long time.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:10.130
So, the best example I know of it is: I read
this book a while back written by a KGB officer

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:14.450
and it was published after the fall of the,
you know, wall in the Soviet Empire.

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:19.839
And this KGB officer claimed that he had worked
in a biological warfare unit in the Soviet

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:25.990
Union, and the purpose of the biological warfare
unit was to turn infectious diseases into

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:30.330
aerosols so that they could be sprayed on
enemy populations.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:35.390
And so they were working on two things: developing
a very virulent infections—and a virulent

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:40.730
infection is one that’s severe but also
very transmissible, because those things are

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:45.000
separate—and also one that could survive
being aerosolized because it turns out that

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.250
that’s quite difficult, which we should
be all be very thankful about.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:54.200
And one of the products they were working
on was an attempt to cross Ebola with smallpox.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:13:01.660
Now Ebola is—you all know about Ebola I
presume—it’s very very fatal, although

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:05.230
they’ve learned ways to treat it now and
maybe have a vaccine.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.920
But at that time it was very very fatal.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.570
And smallpox is incredibly contagious, and
so they were hoping they could develop something

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:20.790
that was very very contagious and very very
deadly that you could spray on civilian populations.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:29.269
Now, you know, here’s where it gets tricky,
and it gets tricky here because how you answer

00:13:29.269 --> 00:13:35.580
this question inevitably depends on how you
define truth.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.870
And truth is actually something you have to
define before you can have a conversation

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:45.360
about it, which is a kind of a strange thing.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:52.850
Anyways, you can make a case that determining
how to cross Ebola with smallpox and aerosolize

00:13:52.850 --> 00:14:02.370
it is a perfectly valid scientific question,
and because science is value free it’s very

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.370
difficult to make an argument that that’s
any worse experiment than any other kind of

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:08.279
experiment or any other kind of investigation.

00:14:08.279 --> 00:14:13.760
And you can also imagine even that if didn’t
go to the aerosolization stage that positive

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:14.959
things might come out of it.

00:14:14.959 --> 00:14:19.649
Maybe you’d end up with a more detailed
understanding of DNA or something as a consequence

00:14:19.649 --> 00:14:22.839
of the investigation that would have positive
consequences.

00:14:22.839 --> 00:14:33.760
But it still seems that—the idea of doing
scientific research to cross Ebola with smallpox—there

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:41.560
seems to be something wrong with that, and
it’s maybe that sense of wrongness as well

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:45.649
that can’t be captured within scientific
models.

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:52.680
And so one of the things that Jung claimed—and
Rogers and the phenomenologists—is that,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:15:00.170
in some sense, is that the scientific reality
has to be encapsulated within an ethical reality

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.180
rather than the other way around.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:06.580
Now Rogers would say, “You know that feeling
that you have when you hear about someone

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:11.360
crossing Ebola with smallpox; that feeling
you have in your body.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:18.160
That’s a real thing, that feeling, and that’s
part of being.”

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:28.750
Now, for the phenomenologists, being is actually—it’s
complicated to define and it’s hard to understand.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:32.139
But what the phenomenologists do is start
with a different set of assumptions.

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:37.889
They don’t assume that the best way to approach
reality is to break it up into the subjective

00:15:37.889 --> 00:15:39.130
and the objective.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:45.070
They presume instead that the way to approach
reality is by assuming that all your experience

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.550
is real.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:47.550
All of it.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:51.050
Now that doesn’t mean there aren’t different
categories of experience, you know, because

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:57.540
you could still say that a dream and the experience
you have walking—dreaming of walking down

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:02.589
the street and walking down the street are
still different things, and the phenomenologists

00:16:02.589 --> 00:16:08.430
would say, “Yes, you can keep your categorical
distinctions intact, but you have to say that

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:11.040
they’re both real.”

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.440
Now, this is an assumption and I told you
why the assumption is generated.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.519
Part of the reason the assumption is generated—the
alternative assumption—is because there

00:16:19.519 --> 00:16:25.620
are problems that emerge with the set of assumptions
that make up the scientific worldview.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:30.790
Now it’s not that surprising that the scientific
worldview can’t encompass the subjective

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:38.070
because the scientific worldview was actually
established—by Bacon and Decartes say and

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:46.850
by other thinkers of that time—it was set
up to exclude the subjective from the conceptualization

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.270
of objective reality.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:52.709
So you can’t really criticize science because
it does that, but you can say the fact that

00:16:52.709 --> 00:17:00.370
the subjective is excluded is a fault, or
at least an incompleteness, and that incompleteness

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:06.920
sometimes has extraordinarily serious consequences
that you can’t just dismiss.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:13.200
Because you might say, “Well, had the Russians—the
Soviets—been successful in crossing Ebola

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:18.100
with smallpox and aerosolizing it then we
might all be dead,” and then you might think

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:26.540
that any theory that leads everyone to be
dead might reasonably be criticized for its

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.570
assumption to universal truth.

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:35.770
Now, you can debate that and rightly so, but
all I’m pointing out is there are reasons

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:38.240
to go at this from another approach.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:48.420
Now, it was Rogers’ basic assumption that
you were more likely to be properly oriented

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:57.210
in life if you adopted a phenomenological
approach to your experience and to break up

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.690
reality in that manner.

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:04.380
So let me give you an example.

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:10.870
When my daughter was about three, she had
a nightmare and she came running into her

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.170
parents’ bedroom.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:20.390
And she told me her dream and she was all
upset by it.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:23.671
She might’ve even been younger than three
at that point.

00:18:23.671 --> 00:18:29.590
And she had dreamed that there was a clear
stream and that it was quite beautiful and

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.919
that there was garbage littering the bottom
of it.

00:18:32.919 --> 00:18:37.070
Now by that time, you know, she—kids are
quite sophisticated in their ability to categorize,

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:38.070
you know.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.590
By that time she’d already learned the difference
between kid things and adult things, and you

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:48.549
think, that’s a bloody complex form of discrimination,
you know, because what objective features

00:18:48.549 --> 00:18:52.880
do adult things share in common that differentiates
them from kid things?

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:57.110
You can’t actually come up with a set of
objective criteria that allows that distinction

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:01.840
to be made, but kids, by the time they’re
three, have got a pretty clear grasp of that.

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.910
So they can come up with very sophisticated
classifications.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:11.480
About the same time, one of my friends had
a two-year-old who went outside their house

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:14.669
one day, looked down the street, and there
were a couple of garbage cans knocked over.

00:19:14.669 --> 00:19:16.520
And the kid said, “Uh oh.”

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:20.910
And, you know, that’s also extremely sophisticated
because you think, you know, a whole street

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:27.620
is a very complex set of visual stimuli and
to be able to glance at that and then to note

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:31.929
that something is out of place and then to
make an utterance of it to draw people’s

00:19:31.929 --> 00:19:36.679
attention to it is a ridiculously sophisticated
cognitive act.

00:19:36.679 --> 00:19:40.120
And my daughter was doing the same thing within
the context of her dream.

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:42.000
So she was upset about this.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.340
Now, it’s quite interested, eh, because
you think, “Well, why would someone who

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:53.200
was three be upset about the fact that a stream—a
beautiful, clear stream—had garbage in it?”

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:58.880
You know, it’s a really—it’s like innate
environmentalism or something like that.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:02.390
Innate concern for the environment and that
could easily be the case, you know.

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:08.370
I mean if we fouled our environment up continually
during our evolutionary history that was likely

00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:09.940
to have very negative consequences.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:13.600
Even birds clean their own nests and so do
bees and, you know, they keep their hives

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:14.600
clean and all that.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.950
So it could be a very primordial instinct.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:21.480
Anyways, she came in with this dream and she
was quite upset.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.250
And then you think there are a couple of things
you could do about that.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:27.330
You could say, “It’s just a dream.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.150
Don’t worry about it.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:34.620
Go back to sleep,” but, you know, that’s
a philosophical statement, right, and it’s

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:40.299
a misleading one, or you could argue that
it was a misleading one, because what does

00:20:40.299 --> 00:20:42.760
“It’s just a dream mean?”

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:48.990
Well, it means that it’s certainly not something
to take seriously like you would take something

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:51.520
that occurred during the day seriously.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:21:05.880
It’s a mere fantasy but with the definition
of fantasy being some fundamentally useless

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:13.191
act of play, I suppose, that you can ignore
with no consequences, and not only that that

00:21:13.191 --> 00:21:14.790
you should ignore it.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:22.840
And, you know, that’s not necessarily true
because then you’re making the case, or

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.960
you might be making the case, that you don’t
have to pay attention to products of your

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:28.580
imagination.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:33.030
That seems like a pretty dopey thing to tell
a child given A, that anything creative that

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:38.100
you ever produce is going to come out of your
imagination and B, the way that you set goals

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:42.539
and plans for your future is by imagining
them—dreaming about them, right?

00:21:42.539 --> 00:21:44.610
Everyone knows what it means to pursue a dream.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:48.140
And, you know, you might say, “Well, if
you’re depressed it means all you dreams

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:49.200
have disappeared.”

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:55.350
It’s like, so maybe you shouldn’t be telling
your child that it’s just a dream and so

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.741
you can ignore it and go back to sleep, you
know.

00:21:57.741 --> 00:22:01.750
You don’t know exactly what you’re doing
when you do that.

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:07.059
You’re certainly not paying attention to
the child’s experience because she did have

00:22:07.059 --> 00:22:10.650
that image and it did make her upset, and
those are facts.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.809
And as far as people like Rogers would be
concerned, those are facts about reality,

00:22:13.809 --> 00:22:19.130
or they’re elements of reality, and they
should be dealt with as such.

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:26.690
So, I told her to close her eyes and to bring
the dream image up in her imagination, which

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:33.919
she could do very well, and then to imagine
that she had some rubber boots on and that

00:22:33.919 --> 00:22:37.790
she could go into the stream and pick out
the garbage and put it in a garbage bag and

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:40.770
that there was a garbage can nearby and that
she could put it in the garbage can.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:46.220
And so we went through that and that calmed
her down and then she went back to bed.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:54.080
And then you might say, “Well, what am I
doing doing that?”

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.670
Because you might say, “Well, that’s just
a fantasy, just like the dream, and because

00:22:56.670 --> 00:23:00.160
they’re just fantasies neither of them have
anything to do with reality.”

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:05.860
But, you know, that again is not a very sophisticated
way of thinking because the dream presented

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:09.630
my daughter with a problem, or you could even
say a class of problems.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:17.460
And the class of problems includes all of
those things that people do, and other animals,

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:21.610
of course—all the ways that the environment
can be defiled.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:26.700
Well, that actually is an existential problem.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.260
And then you might say, “Well, what should
you do about it?”

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.840
and the answer to that might be, “Well,
maybe you should do something about it insofar

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:38.210
as you can do something about it,” because
then you’re conceptualizing yourself as

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:44.390
an active agent and not just a passive victim
or a passive recipient of the problem.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:48.230
You could actually do something about it,
and that’s a way different message than

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:52.630
“It’s just a dream; you can ignore it
and go back to sleep.”

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:55.870
You know, maybe you shouldn’t actually tell
your child to be ignoring reality and go back

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:56.870
to sleep.

00:23:56.870 --> 00:24:00.950
Maybe you should be telling them to wake up,
and see reality as clearly as they possibly

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:05.290
can, and not ignore any of it including their
fantasies and their dreams.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:10.260
And that doesn’t mean that their fantasies
have the same reality as the reality that

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:15.420
you experience with everyone else, but that
fact that it’s not the same reality that

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:20.030
you experience with everyone else doesn’t
mean it’s not reality.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:31.900
Now Rogers would claim, for example, that
to ignore a private experience warps you fundamentally.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:37.790
So, here are the basic outlines of his view
of the world.

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:42.530
So there’s first of all: You exist within
phenomenological field.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:48.919
Now, phenomenon comes from the Greek word
“phainein” and phainein means to shine

00:24:48.919 --> 00:24:50.850
forth.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:55.600
And so phenomena are those things that shine
forth for you.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:25:02.720
Now, the way to understand that is to imagine
that you’re reading a book and maybe a passage

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:05.070
of it really strikes you as meaningful.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:07.870
Well, then you might say, “Well, why?”

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.330
There are lots of passages in the book.

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:16.010
If someone else was reading the book it’s
not obvious that the same passage would strike

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:17.140
them.

00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:21.919
And then you might say, “Well, is that a
consequence of the structure of you as an

00:25:21.919 --> 00:25:26.049
interpreter of the book or is it a consequence
of the contents of the book?” and the answer

00:25:26.049 --> 00:25:32.020
to that is you actually can’t tell, because
the book is neither the thing that’s printed

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:33.020
nor the reader.

00:25:33.020 --> 00:25:39.000
The book is the interaction between the reader
and the book, and that interaction is actually

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:43.070
unique for each person even though there are
some commonalities across people—because

00:25:43.070 --> 00:25:48.429
otherwise you wouldn’t even be able to talk
about a book.

00:25:48.429 --> 00:25:56.170
Well, regardless, when you’re interacting
with someone some things announce themselves

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:01.200
on your field of consciousness and some things
don’t and so the phenomenologists would

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:05.330
say, “Well, some things shine forth for
you while other things don’t.”

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:14.420
It’s a little bit like one of Jung’s ideas
about the development of the self.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:19.080
Jung believed that if you followed things
that if you followed things that announced

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:26.290
themselves to you as meaningful, that they
would take you through a descent to begin

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:31.070
with—where you would encounter things you
were afraid of or terrified of and disgusted

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:35.610
by and wanted to avoid—and then the consequence
of that would be that you would incorporate

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:38.220
a lot of new material into your personality.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:42.900
And the consequence of that would be you’d
be reborn, roughly speaking, as a stronger

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:46.500
person, and then if you just kept doing that
all the time for the rest of your life in

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:51.720
small or large ways that you’d become more
and more and more fully differentiated and

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:52.720
developed.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:59.740
And he would have said that the ultimate potential
target of that kind of development was what

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:02.030
he described as the self.

00:27:02.030 --> 00:27:09.480
The self is a multi-faceted conceptualization,
so that’s not all there is to it, but the

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:13.890
self, from the Jungian perspective is who
you could be if you could be who you most

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:21.860
deeply are; if you didn’t shy away from
anything; if you accurately encountered and

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:25.789
then integrated, even in the Piagetian sense,
your experience.

00:27:25.789 --> 00:27:29.929
Now that’s exactly the same idea that Rogers
had.

00:27:29.929 --> 00:27:36.360
He believed that there’s a phenomenal field
and it’s full of experience.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:40.150
All of your experience manifests itself as
the phenomenal field.

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:43.970
It’s quite interesting because that means
also that other people are part of your phenomenal

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:47.590
field—part of your experience.

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:54.850
And then within that field there’s a circumscribed
area that he described as the self.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:56.930
Now that’s different than the Jungian self.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.630
So it’s a little terminologically confusing.

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:05.600
Rogers’ self is more like Freud’s ego,
but Rogers would define the self as though

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:15.340
aspects of the phenomenological field that
you call “I.” “I felt like…”

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:23.580
I’ve had clients who tended to talk in the
third person about their emotions, or they

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:25.270
would say, “The body.”

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:33.170
They wouldn’t say I. Maybe they’re describing
cutting themselves and they would say, “Well,

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:36.340
the body needed to be cut.”

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:45.100
And what Rogers would say about that was that
the person existed in the phenomenological

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:52.190
field and part of that phenomenological field
was demarcated by the self but the self hadn’t

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:57.100
expanded to incorporate as much of that phenomenological
field as it should’ve.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:03.380
Another concept that he had was the concept
of the organism.

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:09.890
Now, you could think of the self as what you
identify as “I,” and then you can think

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:15.169
of the organism as all those things that are
unique to you that you might identify as the

00:29:15.169 --> 00:29:16.169
“I.”

00:29:16.169 --> 00:29:20.500
Now it’s tricky, eh, because imagine that
you get angry during a fight with someone—argument—and

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:24.660
they say, “You’re angry,” and you say,
“No, I’m not.”

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:27.400
It’s like, what exactly are you doing there?

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:30.529
The other person—we’re going to assume
the other person’s right for the sake of

00:29:30.529 --> 00:29:39.770
this argument—they can clearly see that
you’re angry but you’re not allowing the

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:45.370
organismic manifestation of that motivational
state or that emotion to be incorporated with

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:49.770
those things that you identify with.

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:59.539
Rogers would call that incongruence, and he
believed that incongruence was roughly equivalent

00:29:59.539 --> 00:30:14.350
to psychopathology so that part of your goal
was for the organism to respond appropriately

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:19.210
to the broader phenomenological field—now
we can talk about what appropriately means,

00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:23.150
but we won’t do that yet—and then what
the self was supposed to do was accurately

00:30:23.150 --> 00:30:30.010
symbolically represent organismic responses
to the broader phenomenological field.

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:36.059
So, now you can see that there are similarities
to that concept as say Freud’s idea of the

00:30:36.059 --> 00:30:37.470
ego and the Id.

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:42.970
You know, because the Id was this sort of
external thing, which was home of motivational

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:50.510
and emotional forces that couldn’t be easily
integrated into the ego and partly because

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:53.679
the superego said, for example, that they
were wrong.

00:30:53.679 --> 00:30:58.240
So there are these sets of phenomena, you
know, that all of the different clinicians

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:03.240
are talking about, but all of them are talking
about it starting from different initial presuppositions.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:08.780
It’s like a group of people in a room shining
a flashlight on a very complex object.

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:11.309
You know, they’re all standing in a slightly
different position.

00:31:11.309 --> 00:31:15.899
The complex object is the same thing but you
can only see parts of it so you need multiple

00:31:15.899 --> 00:31:22.110
different ways of looking at things so you
can come up with a cohesive and coherent account

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:26.390
of something that’s actually beyond your
understanding because, of course, not only

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:34.309
can you not accurately symbolically represent
the phenomenological—the entire domain of

00:31:34.309 --> 00:31:43.230
your being—you can’t accurately represent
even the aspect of that field that’s related

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:45.470
to the organismic you.

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:52.220
There’s part of you on top that you identify
with “I,” roughly speaking, and it’s

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:57.120
trying to model a model of something very
complex and, of course, that’s extraordinarily

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:58.120
difficult.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.779
Now, why is it doing that?

00:32:00.779 --> 00:32:05.090
That’s the central mystery of consciousness,
you know.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:10.149
There’s this problem that consciousness
researchers always refer to.

00:32:10.149 --> 00:32:12.159
It’s annoyingly termed I think.

00:32:12.159 --> 00:32:18.570
It’s called the “Zombie Problem,” and
the Zombie problem is: Why is consciousness

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:20.590
necessary?

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:26.900
Why is it necessary for something complex
like a human being to actually have awareness

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:27.900
and self-awareness?

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:33.350
Why couldn’t it just wander through the
world reacting in pre-determined, deterministic

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.430
fashion zombie-like.

00:32:35.430 --> 00:32:39.590
No awareness—robot-like, let’s say.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:45.649
What’s consciousness for?

00:32:45.649 --> 00:32:50.310
Rogers doesn’t really discuss that and so
we won’t at the moment although I think

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:53.539
there are some reasonable ideas.

00:32:53.539 --> 00:32:59.100
Consciousness, for example, allows you to
generalize between certain phenomena.

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:05.700
Anyways, regardless, there are the self, the
organism, and the broader phenomenological

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:09.490
field.

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:21.960
Let’s say you go into a pub—bar—and
you’ve been there many times and so you

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:25.510
know the people in there, you know where to
sit, you know what’s going to happen, you’ve

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:26.669
met friends there.

00:33:26.669 --> 00:33:34.610
You might say that your organism exists, at
least in part, as a very accurate model of

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:37.539
that broader phenomenological world.

00:33:37.539 --> 00:33:50.610
And then, inside that, you’re aware of your
knowledge of the bar, and so if your organism

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:58.230
is properly adapted to the broader phenomenological
field, when you go into the bar or other familiar

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:05.580
place, things that you expect and want happen
instead of things that neither expect or want.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:10.700
And you could say, “Well, perhaps that’s
a reasonable definition of adaptation.”

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:15.929
Now, this isn’t biological or evolutionary
adaptation; this is personal adaptation.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:20.019
They’re similar in some ways but importantly
different.

00:34:20.019 --> 00:34:24.990
I mean you could be adapted to an environment
without that in any way having any effect

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:29.379
whatsoever on your evolutionary adaptiveness.

00:34:29.379 --> 00:34:40.500
Now, if maybe you’re not very well organismically-adapted
to the environment—you have a model of it—and

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:45.149
because of that when you go somewhere familiar,
people don’t like you as much as you think

00:34:45.149 --> 00:34:50.149
they do and you’re not nearly as much of
a master of that situation as you think you

00:34:50.149 --> 00:34:51.149
are.

00:34:51.149 --> 00:34:55.529
And one of the consequences of that—so that
would mean that your self-representation is

00:34:55.529 --> 00:35:01.519
too rigid and simplistic and full of defenses
to notice the discrepancy between your organism

00:35:01.519 --> 00:35:04.819
and the broader environment, and that’s
pathological.

00:35:04.819 --> 00:35:09.260
And the way that that’s going to be marked
is, first, by anxiety.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:11.959
You know, maybe you tell a joke—there are
some people sitting around you—you tell

00:35:11.959 --> 00:35:16.020
a joke and you see people kind of, you know,
making that face, and maybe rolling their

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:17.020
eyes.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:20.119
It’s like, well that’s not exactly—you
might think, “Well, they’re joking.

00:35:20.119 --> 00:35:21.119
You know, they’re just teasing me.”

00:35:21.119 --> 00:35:24.359
Well, maybe they’re not; maybe they’re
damn sick of your stupid stories, and you’ve

00:35:24.359 --> 00:35:27.440
told them fifty times, and you’re not very
funny, and they’d rather you weren’t in

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:29.369
the bar at all.

00:35:29.369 --> 00:35:34.180
And it may be that you’ll actually notice
that, but you don’t necessarily notice it

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:38.010
in a fully symbolically-developed way.

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:44.650
Now, what that would mean is maybe you start
feeling anxiety, and Rogers identifies different

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:47.319
levels of feeling.

00:35:47.319 --> 00:35:53.089
It might start with the subception, and to
subcede something, which is a word that I

00:35:53.089 --> 00:36:03.460
guess comes from general semantics, is to
have the event affect the way that you’re

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:08.109
interacting with the world in ways that you
don’t necessarily notice.

00:36:08.109 --> 00:36:13.230
So it’s sort of equivalent to the idea of
subliminal when you talk about advertising.

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:18.059
A message is flashed at you and it subtly
changes the way that you interact with the

00:36:18.059 --> 00:36:19.289
world but you don’t notice that.

00:36:19.289 --> 00:36:20.859
And that happens to people all the time.

00:36:20.859 --> 00:36:23.339
I mean it’s actually a part of our normal
cognitive structure.

00:36:23.339 --> 00:36:28.420
I mean when you walk into this room, the room
tells you all sorts of things about how to

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:33.160
behave, and you don’t notice that the room
is telling you those things because you know

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:36.009
what it’s telling you so well that you just
act it out.

00:36:36.009 --> 00:36:40.839
You don’t need to go through all the complex
processes of actually perceiving and thinking

00:36:40.839 --> 00:36:41.839
about it.

00:36:41.839 --> 00:36:48.200
And so Rogers’ point is that you can detect
things organismically that will affect the

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:53.019
nature of your experience without necessarily
either even perceiving that or definitely

00:36:53.019 --> 00:36:55.579
not symbolically representing it.

00:36:55.579 --> 00:37:01.710
Now, maybe the social discomfort that’s
inherent in the situation rises up to the

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:02.829
level of perception.

00:37:02.829 --> 00:37:05.239
That’s not symbolization yet.

00:37:05.239 --> 00:37:10.349
Then you might think, “Well, what does it
feel like to be anxious?”

00:37:10.349 --> 00:37:15.910
Well, and I’m not asking, “What are the
objective markers of being anxious?” because

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:17.630
we actually don’t know those.

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:20.799
We can kind of infer them.

00:37:20.799 --> 00:37:24.720
Sometimes people’s heart rate goes up, but
sometimes it decelerates, and then there are

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:29.269
facial expressions that are associated with
anxiety but you have to use those to infer.

00:37:29.269 --> 00:37:33.210
If you get anxious, I don’t feel your anxiety;
I have to infer it.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:35.869
But if you get anxious, you feel your anxiety.

00:37:35.869 --> 00:37:39.700
And so then the question might be, “Well,
what does it mean to feel anxious?”

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:45.109
Well, that’s a hard question, and the reason
it’s a hard question is because you never

00:37:45.109 --> 00:37:49.779
have to answer it because if you tell someone,
“I feel anxious,” then they refer to their

00:37:49.779 --> 00:37:52.660
own feelings of anxiety and then they know
what you mean.

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:58.440
And you never have to come up with an explanation
of what it feels like.

00:37:58.440 --> 00:38:03.289
The idea that the feeling is an element of
experience is something that we just take

00:38:03.289 --> 00:38:04.529
for granted, you know.

00:38:04.529 --> 00:38:05.609
“I got angry today.”

00:38:05.609 --> 00:38:06.609
Everybody nods.

00:38:06.609 --> 00:38:09.999
Or maybe I tell a little story and I say,
“Well, you know, I got on a bus and I was

00:38:09.999 --> 00:38:14.269
missing like two cents, and I asked the bus
driver if he would let me get on the bus and

00:38:14.269 --> 00:38:15.269
he didn’t.

00:38:15.269 --> 00:38:19.269
And that really annoyed me because I take
that bus everyday and the driver knows me.

00:38:19.269 --> 00:38:21.309
And I got angry,” and everybody’ll nod.

00:38:21.309 --> 00:38:25.569
And the reason they nod is, well partly because
they can imagine that that’s how they would’ve

00:38:25.569 --> 00:38:31.769
reacted in that situation, but even more importantly,
they accept the reality of angry as a response

00:38:31.769 --> 00:38:34.430
and they know what it feels like.

00:38:34.430 --> 00:38:41.420
So, but explaining it: that’s a much more
difficult thing.

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:46.369
So the point is is that you can have experiences
that you know of—first of all you can have

00:38:46.369 --> 00:38:47.869
experiences that you don’t know of.

00:38:47.869 --> 00:38:49.750
That would be the subceived experiences.

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:54.709
Then you can have experiences that you perceive
but that you haven’t symbolically represented,

00:38:54.709 --> 00:38:57.829
and those might be emotional or motivational
experiences.

00:38:57.829 --> 00:39:02.430
And then you can have experiences that you
have symbolically represented in a way that

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:08.009
captures their gist accurately in a way that’s
reflective or your adaptation, or lack thereof,

00:39:08.009 --> 00:39:10.660
of the broader phenomenological world.

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:16.599
And Rogers would say that latter state is
preferable.

00:39:16.599 --> 00:39:22.910
You know how to act or you know that you didn’t
act properly and you know how to fix it, which

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:27.509
is almost as good, and you represent that
accurately.

00:39:27.509 --> 00:39:33.839
Now, you might imagine that you could concentrate
on the mismatch between organismic responses

00:39:33.839 --> 00:39:35.140
and the environment.

00:39:35.140 --> 00:39:38.150
That’s what behavior therapist really do.

00:39:38.150 --> 00:39:42.549
They say, “Well, how are you acting and
what are the consequences?”

00:39:42.549 --> 00:39:45.959
But that isn’t really where Rogers goes.

00:39:45.959 --> 00:39:51.819
Rogers is more psychoanalytic than the behavior
therapists, and what he seems to be primarily

00:39:51.819 --> 00:39:59.079
concerned about is whether or not your self-representation
is an accurate representation of how your

00:39:59.079 --> 00:40:00.569
organism is responding.

00:40:00.569 --> 00:40:05.449
In some sense, for Rogers, the organism takes
the place of God.

00:40:05.449 --> 00:40:09.339
It’s more or less omniscient.

00:40:09.339 --> 00:40:14.640
So your organism is going to tell you the
truth in every situation and it’s up to

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:19.890
you whether or not your going to pay attention
to that and make sense of it or whether you’re

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:25.930
going to engage in any of the different kinds
of shenanigans that you might engage in to

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:31.619
stop yourself from going through the complex
process of symbolization.

00:40:31.619 --> 00:40:36.009
Because you might say—well, you’re back
in the bar—and you might say, well, you

00:40:36.009 --> 00:40:41.549
noticed the anxiety and then, you know, different
ways of symbolizing it come to mind.

00:40:41.549 --> 00:40:47.650
“These people are all just jerks,” and
then another thing that comes to mind is,

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:49.910
“Well, maybe nobody likes me.”

00:40:49.910 --> 00:40:56.299
And then maybe another idea that’s associated
with symbolization comes to mind that is,

00:40:56.299 --> 00:41:02.309
“Well, maybe there are elements of my social
behavior that I should adjust,” and I presume

00:41:02.309 --> 00:41:08.160
that if Rogers was having a conversation with
one of his clients he would assume that that

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:13.170
was the most fruitful potential step towards
accurate symbolization.

00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:22.270
Now, Rogers felt that anxiety emerged when
the self-concept was not accurately symbolically

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:27.640
representing the organism’s experiences,
and so that would be, in some sense: If you’re

00:41:27.640 --> 00:41:35.130
a house divided within yourself then you’re
pathological from a Rogerian perspective.

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:42.249
And you can think about that, in some sense,
as a move away from necessary simplicity.

00:41:42.249 --> 00:41:50.680
I mean if your self-concept is set up properly
it doesn’t contain contradictory and misleading

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:53.509
bits of information; everything works seamlessly
together.

00:41:53.509 --> 00:41:59.420
And so what that would mean is that you think
about something, you act it out, and it works.

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:05.450
And then that’s an indication that the organization
between, say, these three different levels

00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:09.829
of being is—well the right word is isomorphic.

00:42:09.829 --> 00:42:13.229
Each represents the other with sufficient
accuracy.

00:42:13.229 --> 00:42:20.349
And so that means that you’re congruent,
and if you’re congruent then the world is

00:42:20.349 --> 00:42:25.779
treating you not only in the manner that you
want to be treated but also in the manner

00:42:25.779 --> 00:42:34.049
that’s most likely to further your action
in the world as an organism.

00:42:34.049 --> 00:42:38.709
And so that’s partly why Rogers believed
that the organism had wisdom: it’s because

00:42:38.709 --> 00:42:46.130
its natural proclivity was to operate in the
world effectively and to expand its competence

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:47.700
at the same time.

00:42:47.700 --> 00:42:54.230
Now, a self-actualized person—because that’s
one of Rogers’ phrases—is someone whose

00:42:54.230 --> 00:43:01.319
self-concept accurately represents their organismic
being.

00:43:01.319 --> 00:43:14.199
Now, Rogers was primarily a clinician as well
and his theories were derived from his clinical

00:43:14.199 --> 00:43:16.479
experience.

00:43:16.479 --> 00:43:21.479
And his theories informed his clinical practice.

00:43:21.479 --> 00:43:27.950
And Rogers invented, or developed, what he
believed to be a new kind of therapy and he

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:30.050
called that “client-centered therapy.”

00:43:30.050 --> 00:43:34.130
Now you’ve probably heard about student-centered
education.

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:38.160
I mean Rogers’ ideas have had a tremendous
amount of influence, certainly not least in

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:43.809
educational psychology—also in conflict
reduction of various sorts.

00:43:43.809 --> 00:43:50.869
If you start with Rogers’ initial assumptions,
which are, first of all, that you should regard

00:43:50.869 --> 00:43:56.749
your experience as real; second, that your
organism will give you accurate information

00:43:56.749 --> 00:44:05.559
about the totality of experience; and third,
that your self-concept should accurately encompass

00:44:05.559 --> 00:44:11.089
your actual experience, there are implications
of that theory for therapy.

00:44:11.089 --> 00:44:16.520
And one of the implications is that you can
figure out what you should do if you want

00:44:16.520 --> 00:44:25.989
to because your organism will tell you, or
at least hint at you, about which directions

00:44:25.989 --> 00:44:31.839
are correct and which directions aren’t,
and if you follow those then you’re going

00:44:31.839 --> 00:44:36.150
to naturally gravitate towards a state of
being where you’re more and more accurately

00:44:36.150 --> 00:44:42.509
represented by yourself but also that your
representation and your organism are going

00:44:42.509 --> 00:44:44.369
to act more effectively in the world.

00:44:44.369 --> 00:44:47.469
They’re not bumping up against each other
all the time.

00:44:47.469 --> 00:44:55.589
And, you know, they’re not in conflict anymore,
and so that’s the goal of the self-actualized

00:44:55.589 --> 00:44:59.309
person.

00:44:59.309 --> 00:45:08.549
And therapy, in part, is the art of furthering
that process of self-actualization.

00:45:08.549 --> 00:45:27.069
Now, Rogers was opposed to diagnosis because
he felt that it didn’t sufficiently represent

00:45:27.069 --> 00:45:40.319
the vital minutia of the client’s individual
experience, and I think that his claim was

00:45:40.319 --> 00:45:52.339
extremely important, you know, because medicine—psychiatry—tends
to classify everything.

00:45:52.339 --> 00:45:58.329
And you might say, “Well, are the psychiatric
diagnostic classifications real?” and then

00:45:58.329 --> 00:46:01.220
the answer to that is: It depends on what
you mean by real.

00:46:01.220 --> 00:46:07.039
And you might think, “Well, that’s an
annoying answer,” but it is still the answer.

00:46:07.039 --> 00:46:13.730
And so I could say if I was being cynical—although
I don’t really think it’s a cynical response—if

00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:20.029
I willing to sound cynical, that the only
reason that psychiatrists diagnose their clients

00:46:20.029 --> 00:46:23.030
is so that insurance companies pay the bills.

00:46:23.030 --> 00:46:27.569
And I actually believe that that’s more
true than the claim that the psychiatric diagnostic

00:46:27.569 --> 00:46:34.829
categories actually capture the essence of
the person’s problems.

00:46:34.829 --> 00:46:42.789
In my clinical experience, classification
has had almost zero utility, and the reason

00:46:42.789 --> 00:46:45.049
for that is because it’s too low resolution.

00:46:45.049 --> 00:46:51.489
It doesn’t capture the individual properly
because the thing about a client—a clinical

00:46:51.489 --> 00:46:57.209
client—is that it’s precisely their individuality
that’s the fundamental issue.

00:46:57.209 --> 00:47:02.950
It’s like, they have to make their way through
the world in their body, in their time, in

00:47:02.950 --> 00:47:13.769
their position in culture, with their advantages
and disadvantages, and that requires a solution

00:47:13.769 --> 00:47:21.319
that’s unique in many ways and one that’s
also personal.

00:47:21.319 --> 00:47:30.589
And Rogers also wasn’t a particularly great
fan of the psychoanalysts’ tendency to look

00:47:30.589 --> 00:47:36.769
for the causes of present behavior in past
events.

00:47:36.769 --> 00:47:46.289
So he didn’t regard fishing in the past
as a necessarily useful expedition, or as

00:47:46.289 --> 00:47:54.740
a necessary expedition, although if issues
of the past came up in therapy he was perfectly

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:57.219
willing to deal with them.

00:47:57.219 --> 00:48:03.529
He didn’t accept the Freudian theory that
the fundamental cause of psychopathology was,

00:48:03.529 --> 00:48:09.369
say, traumatic past experience.

00:48:09.369 --> 00:48:18.140
Now, this gives Rogerian therapy a particular
flavor.

00:48:18.140 --> 00:48:20.119
You could also call it “Non-directive therapy.”

00:48:20.119 --> 00:48:31.999
Now, if you’re a behavior therapist, you
might actively strategize with your client

00:48:31.999 --> 00:48:37.760
about what they’re going to do in the next
week and once they establish their goals you

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:44.180
might offer some hints about what strategies
they could use to monitor themselves and to

00:48:44.180 --> 00:48:47.970
attain those goals, and maybe you’ll even
have them keep track of it.

00:48:47.970 --> 00:48:51.519
Rogers, generally speaking, wouldn’t do
something that directive.

00:48:51.519 --> 00:48:59.770
In fact, he strove not to unduly influence
the client’s decisions about important future

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:07.589
events, and in that way he was quite similar
to Freud, because Freud would sit so the client

00:49:07.589 --> 00:49:12.640
would be laying on a couch and Freud so that
the client—his patient actually—couldn’t

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:13.640
see him.

00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:17.869
And then he would listen to the person free-associate,
which in some sense means that they would

00:49:17.869 --> 00:49:27.849
ramble on, and he was attempting by doing
that to facilitate the self-realization of

00:49:27.849 --> 00:49:31.099
their own memories.

00:49:31.099 --> 00:49:34.420
If things happened to you in the past and
they still cause you emotion—let’s say

00:49:34.420 --> 00:49:38.529
they’re more than eighteen months old for
the sake of argument—Freud would assume

00:49:38.529 --> 00:49:44.690
that if you kind of wandered around that memory
somewhat haphazardly and in an associational

00:49:44.690 --> 00:49:50.609
way—the way conversations naturally unfold—that
you would bring things to light and that would

00:49:50.609 --> 00:49:56.150
allow certain kinds of emotional expression,
and that, in fact, in itself would be curative.

00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:59.390
But Freud didn’t want to interfere with
that process or guide it to any great degree,

00:49:59.390 --> 00:50:02.670
and Rogers was like that; he wanted you to
make your own decisions.

00:50:02.670 --> 00:50:15.199
And so he believed that the therapeutic endeavor
could not occur unless certain conditions

00:50:15.199 --> 00:50:24.680
were met, and the conditions were: Willingness
to tell the truth.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:33.349
So when a client comes to see me, one of the
first things I tell them is: “You can tell

00:50:33.349 --> 00:50:40.420
me anything you want—although you’re perfectly
within your rights to maintain your privacy—you

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:45.400
can tell me anything you want but tell me
the truth as clearly as you can because if

00:50:45.400 --> 00:50:50.859
you don’t I don’t know what’s going
on with you and there’s not any possibility

00:50:50.859 --> 00:50:57.559
at all that this process will be useful if
we’re talking about things that don’t

00:50:57.559 --> 00:50:58.559
exist.

00:50:58.559 --> 00:51:02.890
So one condition is the person is willing
to come and see you; so that means they want

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:04.259
to transform.

00:51:04.259 --> 00:51:08.760
The second precondition is that they’re
going to communicate with you, and communication

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:12.780
means they’re going to do their best to
tell the truth but the therapist takes the

00:51:12.780 --> 00:51:17.099
same vow, in a sense.

00:51:17.099 --> 00:51:21.309
And so one of the things that I tell my clients
is that I will never let them do anything

00:51:21.309 --> 00:51:24.829
that makes me resent them.

00:51:24.829 --> 00:51:29.430
So if I’m asked to step out of my way for
some reason to take an additional session

00:51:29.430 --> 00:51:36.269
or maybe to meet them somewhere and I do it,
they can be assured that there won’t be

00:51:36.269 --> 00:51:44.980
a price that will be extracted from them later
for interfering with the smooth flow of my

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:46.079
day.

00:51:46.079 --> 00:51:51.430
So, and the other thing that I do—and I’ve
learned this from Rogers and from the other

00:51:51.430 --> 00:51:56.979
people that I’ve read—is that I’ll listen
to people talk and then sometimes something

00:51:56.979 --> 00:51:59.269
will occur to me.

00:51:59.269 --> 00:52:04.969
And I don’t think about it as I thought
something because I’m actually somewhat

00:52:04.969 --> 00:52:10.170
a follower of Nietzsche’s dictum, which
is that the fact that you think is no proof

00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:16.289
that—the fact that you experience thoughts
is no proof that you thought them.

00:52:16.289 --> 00:52:18.000
They just appeared.

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:22.009
Well, you don’t think that you think up
your dreams, right?

00:52:22.009 --> 00:52:24.289
You have your dreams.

00:52:24.289 --> 00:52:26.369
There you are in bed; there are the dreams.

00:52:26.369 --> 00:52:28.239
Are you having them?

00:52:28.239 --> 00:52:30.099
Well, it doesn’t seem like it.

00:52:30.099 --> 00:52:33.660
It seems like you’re experiencing them,
in some sense, the same way that you experience

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:34.660
the external word.

00:52:34.660 --> 00:52:37.329
You’re not voluntarily creating them.

00:52:37.329 --> 00:52:43.359
Well, why do you think anything different
about your thoughts?

00:52:43.359 --> 00:52:46.770
And that’s a very useful thing to realize,
by the way, because otherwise you think you’re

00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:49.769
your thoughts and then if you have negative
thoughts, well then you assume that they’re

00:52:49.769 --> 00:52:53.789
true and they’re not true just because you
think them.

00:52:53.789 --> 00:52:56.809
They’re just thoughts, and maybe they’re
true and maybe they’re useful and maybe

00:52:56.809 --> 00:52:57.809
they’re not.

00:52:57.809 --> 00:53:02.779
So stepping back from them a little bit is
incredibly—it’s like Buddhist detachment

00:53:02.779 --> 00:53:03.779
in a sense.

00:53:03.779 --> 00:53:05.119
It’s an incredibly useful thing to do.

00:53:05.119 --> 00:53:08.849
I mean that’s another thing that’s really
characteristic of modern people, especially

00:53:08.849 --> 00:53:10.189
if they’re smart.

00:53:10.189 --> 00:53:16.559
They think they think, first of all, and second,
they think that the things they think are

00:53:16.559 --> 00:53:19.190
true, and neither of those things are necessarily
true.

00:53:19.190 --> 00:53:31.979
And abandoning your belief in them makes you
much less rigid and much more able to expand

00:53:31.979 --> 00:53:37.809
your preconceptions or even radically change
them when the circumstances demand them.

00:53:37.809 --> 00:53:47.540
So the person has to be wanting to change,
in principle in a positive direction; they

00:53:47.540 --> 00:53:49.449
have to be willing to tell the truth.

00:53:49.449 --> 00:53:56.180
Then the next element of Rogerian psychotherapy
is something that he called “unconditional

00:53:56.180 --> 00:54:05.999
positive regard,” and unconditional positive
regard, in the Rogerian tradition, is something

00:54:05.999 --> 00:54:08.969
like radical acceptance.

00:54:08.969 --> 00:54:14.670
It’s actually a hallmark of modern child
rearing techniques.

00:54:14.670 --> 00:54:22.819
You should wholeheartedly accept all aspects
of the person’s being.

00:54:22.819 --> 00:54:35.109
Now, Rogers believed that, or posited that,
partly because he also believed in—what

00:54:35.109 --> 00:54:40.670
was it called—conditions of worth.

00:54:40.670 --> 00:54:49.390
Here’s a condition of worth: “I don’t
like you when you act like that.”

00:54:49.390 --> 00:54:54.660
So imagine you tell a child that—“I don’t
like you when you act like that”—and maybe

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:59.430
you tell them that about a bunch of things
that they do.

00:54:59.430 --> 00:55:09.269
But maybe those are integral parts of the
child’s being and by making your lack of

00:55:09.269 --> 00:55:15.799
acceptance known you make it impossible for
the child to incorporate those aspects of

00:55:15.799 --> 00:55:21.369
his organismal experience into his self-concept.

00:55:21.369 --> 00:55:26.489
You can see echoes of the superego/ego idea
there.

00:55:26.489 --> 00:55:31.729
And so Rogers would say, “Well, if someone
comes into psychotherapy and experiences unconditional

00:55:31.729 --> 00:55:37.329
positive regard, that means that they’ll
be able to reveal those elements of their

00:55:37.329 --> 00:55:45.049
organismic experience that had been rejected,
say, by the social world and themselves; experience

00:55:45.049 --> 00:55:49.969
them; and then potentially incorporate them
within the self concept and that that’s

00:55:49.969 --> 00:55:55.679
going to be a good thing, because Rogers is
a great believer in the natural inevitability

00:55:55.679 --> 00:56:02.929
of positive transformation if you set that
up as your goal.

00:56:02.929 --> 00:56:07.289
He also believes that that should be your
goal and that it’s actually your natural

00:56:07.289 --> 00:56:16.069
goal if, for example, you haven’t been subjected
to arbitrary conditions of worth.

00:56:16.069 --> 00:56:25.650
Now, I’m going to read you something that
Rogers wrote, which is quite interesting,

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:29.499
and I think I found it extremely effective.

00:56:29.499 --> 00:56:43.140
Oh yes, that’s another thing I should mention.

00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:55.979
There is one other important assumption in
the idea of self-actualization.

00:56:55.979 --> 00:57:02.439
The other thing that Rogers tried to ensure
that his clients developed is autonomy and

00:57:02.439 --> 00:57:03.439
independence.

00:57:03.439 --> 00:57:12.059
Now, for Rogers, there was very little difference
between the move towards autonomy and personal

00:57:12.059 --> 00:57:16.319
independence and the process of self-actualization.

00:57:16.319 --> 00:57:31.660
Now, given that we’re looking back at Rogers’
work fifty years later, you might ask yourself

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:41.170
if autonomy and independence is a necessary
precondition for psychological health, because

00:57:41.170 --> 00:57:51.480
you could make the case that productive interdependence
and more communal orientation is equally healthy—but

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:57.549
that’s not a Rogerian presupposition.

00:57:57.549 --> 00:58:10.719
So his ideal is the fully-functioning individual.

00:58:10.719 --> 00:58:19.150
So here’s his hypothesis with regards to
psychotherapy: “Assuming a minimal mutual

00:58:19.150 --> 00:58:25.939
willingness to be in contact and to receive
communications, we may say that the greater

00:58:25.939 --> 00:58:30.660
the communicated congruence of experience,
awareness and behavior on the part of one

00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:35.199
individual, the more the ensuing relationship
will involve a tendency toward reciprocal

00:58:35.199 --> 00:58:41.490
communication with the same qualities, mutually
accurate understanding of the communications,

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:46.539
improved psychological adjustment and functioning
in both parties, and mutual satisfaction in

00:58:46.539 --> 00:58:47.730
the relationship.”

00:58:47.730 --> 00:58:50.710
Okay, so let’s unpack that.

00:58:50.710 --> 00:58:54.789
“Assuming a minimal mutual willingness to
be in contact and to receive communications.”

00:58:54.789 --> 00:58:59.479
Well, the first thing you might say is that’s
pretty much the hallmark of a relationship,

00:58:59.479 --> 00:59:04.749
right, because if you’re not in contact,
well clearly you don’t have a relationship,

00:59:04.749 --> 00:59:08.259
and if you are in contact but you’re not
communicating you probably don’t have a

00:59:08.259 --> 00:59:10.259
relationship either.

00:59:10.259 --> 00:59:17.469
So and, you know, that also begs the question
of exactly what a relationship is for, and

00:59:17.469 --> 00:59:20.969
that’s something that’s very much worth
considering because people don’t really

00:59:20.969 --> 00:59:23.199
usually know what a relationship is for.

00:59:23.199 --> 00:59:27.779
They certainly know that as a general rule
they’re going to pursue them but they don’t

00:59:27.779 --> 00:59:32.929
really have any idea, in some sense, what
the optimal state is or what the desired final

00:59:32.929 --> 00:59:34.569
state should be.

00:59:34.569 --> 00:59:43.630
Now of course the desired final state fifty
years ago was marriage, but it’s certainly

00:59:43.630 --> 00:59:47.299
the case now that most people who are couples
aren’t married.

00:59:47.299 --> 00:59:52.569
Now, you might ask yourself, “Well, what’s
the utility of a relationship and what’s

00:59:52.569 --> 00:59:55.170
the utility of a long-term relationship?”

00:59:55.170 --> 01:00:09.710
Well, a Rogerian answer to that would be:
The more based on trust the relationship is,

01:00:09.710 --> 01:00:15.950
the more likely that you’re going to be
in contact and receive communications, and

01:00:15.950 --> 01:00:28.799
then, as a consequence of that, you’re going
to become better and better at representing

01:00:28.799 --> 01:00:35.130
your organismal experience within your self-concept
and that’s going to facilitate your moment

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:37.949
towards self-awareness and self-actualization.

01:00:37.949 --> 01:00:43.499
It’s a two brains are better than one idea.

01:00:43.499 --> 01:00:47.699
So we say, “Well, we established a relationship,
okay, and the goal of our relationship is

01:00:47.699 --> 01:00:50.459
to make things better.”

01:00:50.459 --> 01:00:57.519
And the way we make things better is by telling
the truth as you see it.

01:00:57.519 --> 01:01:03.759
So, remember, this is a phenomenological theory
and so the truth that Rogers is talking about

01:01:03.759 --> 01:01:09.499
isn’t object truth, and I suspect that that’s
at least in part because establishing objective

01:01:09.499 --> 01:01:14.599
truth within the confines of a relationship
is—I think it’s technically impossible.

01:01:14.599 --> 01:01:19.599
And we talked about that before, because each
interaction between the members of a couple,

01:01:19.599 --> 01:01:25.380
or people in any relationship is contaminated,
in a sense, by all of the other interactions

01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:29.959
they’ve ever had plus everything they bring
to bear on the situation, and there’s just

01:01:29.959 --> 01:01:33.179
no untangling that objectively.

01:01:33.179 --> 01:01:34.859
You know, “What did you mean when you said
that?”

01:01:34.859 --> 01:01:43.439
Well, God, you know, you can argue about that
until the point of divorce and ten years afterwards,

01:01:43.439 --> 01:01:46.970
you know, without ever coming to a satisfactory
conclusion.

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:52.309
Alright, “The greater the communicated congruence
of experience, awareness, and behavior on

01:01:52.309 --> 01:01:56.919
the part of one individual, the more the ensuing
relationship will involve a tendency toward

01:01:56.919 --> 01:01:58.839
reciprocal communication with the same qualities.”

01:01:58.839 --> 01:02:03.509
Okay, so here’s the idea: You go see a psychotherapist—a
Rogerian psychotherapist.

01:02:03.509 --> 01:02:09.609
At least in principle, this is someone who’s
mastered, or is attempting to master these

01:02:09.609 --> 01:02:12.509
basic modes of being.

01:02:12.509 --> 01:02:16.630
So what they’re going to do is they’ll
tell you, “I’m going to tell you the truth

01:02:16.630 --> 01:02:22.549
as I see it,” and so they way I see that
happening in psychotherapy is that when someone

01:02:22.549 --> 01:02:29.160
comes to see me I listen to them, and that
means that I’m not distracted by anything

01:02:29.160 --> 01:02:30.160
else.

01:02:30.160 --> 01:02:31.480
I’m not thinking about anything else.

01:02:31.480 --> 01:02:34.250
I’m not thinking about what I’m going
to say next.

01:02:34.250 --> 01:02:36.749
I’m not thinking about the things I have
to do today.

01:02:36.749 --> 01:02:40.930
I’m not thinking about the last time I saw
this person.

01:02:40.930 --> 01:02:46.890
I’m not allowing my attention to wander
away from the person, and then I’m watching

01:02:46.890 --> 01:02:52.219
them and listening to them at the same time—and
those are the same things because to really

01:02:52.219 --> 01:02:54.140
listen to someone you also have to watch them.

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:59.650
Because then you subceive perhaps, or perceive,
all the non-verbal elements of communication

01:02:59.650 --> 01:03:04.140
that go along—well, partly with voice tone
but partly with eye direction and partly with

01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:06.939
very subtle transformations of facial gestures.

01:03:06.939 --> 01:03:11.079
So sometimes, for example, I see on my clients
that they’ll all of a sudden turn into a

01:03:11.079 --> 01:03:12.079
child.

01:03:12.079 --> 01:03:13.079
It’s very interesting.

01:03:13.079 --> 01:03:16.999
It’s like they’ll be recounting some experience—it’s
like fixation from a Freudian perspective.

01:03:16.999 --> 01:03:23.130
They’ll be describing some experience from
the distant past and for a second their expression

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:26.039
will approximate who they were when they were
six-years-old.

01:03:26.039 --> 01:03:30.829
It’s really something remarkable to see,
and you can see these things if you really

01:03:30.829 --> 01:03:32.299
pay attention.

01:03:32.299 --> 01:03:43.319
And then as they talk I’m watching what’s
happening inside me as I listen to their words,

01:03:43.319 --> 01:03:45.900
and all sorts of things will happen.

01:03:45.900 --> 01:03:49.420
Sometimes I’ll get an image or sometimes
I’ll get a memory of something we talked

01:03:49.420 --> 01:03:50.420
about before.

01:03:50.420 --> 01:03:54.440
Or sometimes I’ll notice that what they’re
saying right now is different than something

01:03:54.440 --> 01:03:58.219
they said five minutes ago and so then I’ll
say, “Well, wait a second, you know.

01:03:58.219 --> 01:04:03.109
I’m trying to understand what you’re telling
me and I noticed that you just said this,

01:04:03.109 --> 01:04:06.819
and it seems to imply this, but then, you
know, a couple of minutes ago, when we were

01:04:06.819 --> 01:04:10.949
talking about that, then you said this and
I don’t see how those two things are the

01:04:10.949 --> 01:04:11.949
same.”

01:04:11.949 --> 01:04:19.390
And I’m not doing that because I want them
to rectify the paradox; I’m doing that being

01:04:19.390 --> 01:04:21.319
I want to understand what they are saying.

01:04:21.319 --> 01:04:25.799
And if someone says A to you and then they
say B, you don’t know what they’re saying.

01:04:25.799 --> 01:04:28.509
And so the question is for clarification.

01:04:28.509 --> 01:04:31.539
There’s no other direction than that.

01:04:31.539 --> 01:04:35.660
And then maybe, you know, I’ll have a little
image, like maybe the fragment of a dream,

01:04:35.660 --> 01:04:38.450
and I’ll say, “Wait a second.

01:04:38.450 --> 01:04:41.170
When you said what you just said this is what
I experienced.”

01:04:41.170 --> 01:04:47.209
And then maybe we’ll talk for a while about
what that might mean and I can say to them

01:04:47.209 --> 01:04:53.069
what—so, for example, sometimes people will
come to my office and they’ll do all sorts

01:04:53.069 --> 01:04:54.069
of strange things.

01:04:54.069 --> 01:05:02.489
Like I had one client who had psychosomatic
epilepsy, and one of the first times I saw

01:05:02.489 --> 01:05:05.390
her she had a seizure in my office.

01:05:05.390 --> 01:05:12.460
And I was watching her and it didn’t really
matter to me that she was having a seizure.

01:05:12.460 --> 01:05:15.049
And I thought, “That’s pretty weird,”
because normally when someone’s having a

01:05:15.049 --> 01:05:18.779
seizure it’s like you get affected by it,
right?

01:05:18.779 --> 01:05:24.699
I mean there are sensations that emerge in
your midsection, and there are thoughts of

01:05:24.699 --> 01:05:29.689
emergency, you know, because your body, in
some sense, is mimicking their body—which

01:05:29.689 --> 01:05:33.410
is what your body does all the time when you’re
talking to people—and that produces a sense

01:05:33.410 --> 01:05:34.410
of urgency.

01:05:34.410 --> 01:05:39.450
And I sort of just watched her have this seizure
and I thought, “Oh, it’s not real; it’s

01:05:39.450 --> 01:05:40.660
not a real seizure.”

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:44.339
But she was very good at it and, you know,
as far as she was concerned she couldn’t

01:05:44.339 --> 01:05:46.470
tell if the seizures were real or not.

01:05:46.470 --> 01:05:51.400
And sometimes if you have psychogenic epilepsy
you’ve had an epileptic seizure at one point

01:05:51.400 --> 01:05:52.559
that was genuine.

01:05:52.559 --> 01:05:58.239
Well, it’s exceptionally complicated and
it’s not easy to develop psychogenic epilepsy,

01:05:58.239 --> 01:06:07.769
but she had developed it and part of the reason
was that she thought that—she was toying

01:06:07.769 --> 01:06:14.789
with the idea that existence as a psychiatric
patient would require less effort and responsibility.

01:06:14.789 --> 01:06:19.430
And so she got tangled up at one point with
one of the mental hospitals and they diagnosed

01:06:19.430 --> 01:06:22.749
her with paranoid schizophrenia, which is
not something I would recommend—especially

01:06:22.749 --> 01:06:28.930
if you don’t actually have it—because
once you’ve got the diagnosis, well are

01:06:28.930 --> 01:06:30.039
you really going to know?

01:06:30.039 --> 01:06:31.750
What’s going to happen, eh?

01:06:31.750 --> 01:06:39.189
You do something like that—you have a psychogenic
epileptic seizure and a variety of other symptoms—they’re

01:06:39.189 --> 01:06:43.769
the consequences of a very complex complex
in Jungian terms.

01:06:43.769 --> 01:06:47.630
There’s a secondary motivation in Freudian
terms.

01:06:47.630 --> 01:06:51.470
You go through with the whole routine then
you end up in a psych hospital, and you see

01:06:51.470 --> 01:06:53.859
four experts and they tell you you have paranoid
schizophrenia.

01:06:53.859 --> 01:06:56.609
Do you really think you’re going to not
believe that?

01:06:56.609 --> 01:06:58.349
Or at least ten percent of you is going to
believe it.

01:06:58.349 --> 01:07:00.539
You’re going to wonder after that.

01:07:00.539 --> 01:07:03.969
And if another twenty people tell you that
and you’re put on medication, which you

01:07:03.969 --> 01:07:08.690
could easily be, then a year later, well,
you might still not have paranoid schizophrenia

01:07:08.690 --> 01:07:12.489
but you are not going to be doing very well.

01:07:12.489 --> 01:07:20.439
So, well that’s a good example of what happens
when your self-concept and organismic valuing

01:07:20.439 --> 01:07:23.819
systems aren’t working carefully in tandem.

01:07:23.819 --> 01:07:30.789
Anyways, so someone might be telling me a
story that’s, you know, about something

01:07:30.789 --> 01:07:34.529
terrible that happened to them and I’ll
just be sitting there like a brick wall.

01:07:34.529 --> 01:07:37.560
It’s like, “You’re not affecting me.”

01:07:37.560 --> 01:07:38.869
And sometimes I’ll tell people that.

01:07:38.869 --> 01:07:41.519
It’s like: “I don’t know why, you know,
that’s a pretty terrible story but I’m

01:07:41.519 --> 01:07:44.679
having no emotional response to it at all.

01:07:44.679 --> 01:07:47.529
Do you have any idea why that might be?”

01:07:47.529 --> 01:07:52.631
Well then, you know, often people aren’t
very happy when you tell them that, and that’s

01:07:52.631 --> 01:07:53.631
fine.

01:07:53.631 --> 01:07:54.631
They can be unhappy with that.

01:07:54.631 --> 01:07:57.759
That’s perfectly okay, but the thing is
now they know that there’s at least one

01:07:57.759 --> 01:08:02.380
person that they’ve communicated with that’s
actually telling them the truth who is actually

01:08:02.380 --> 01:08:04.979
not affected by their story.

01:08:04.979 --> 01:08:10.319
Because, look, if someone sits down beside
you and they tell you a terrible story, the

01:08:10.319 --> 01:08:14.039
probability that you’re going to tell them
that that had no effect on you is basically

01:08:14.039 --> 01:08:19.219
zero because it’s really rude not to be
negatively affected by someone who’s telling

01:08:19.219 --> 01:08:22.380
you a very sad story.

01:08:22.380 --> 01:08:24.929
But if you’re negatively affected by it
and it’s not true then you’re not doing

01:08:24.929 --> 01:08:28.630
them any good at all; all you’re doing is
providing them with evidence to maintain what

01:08:28.630 --> 01:08:32.710
could easily become a delusion if they worked
on it long enough.

01:08:32.710 --> 01:08:37.120
By contrast, sometimes people will come into
my office and they’ll tell me something

01:08:37.120 --> 01:08:43.900
that just seems trivial and it’ll make me
tear up, which mean I can actually feel emotions.

01:08:43.900 --> 01:08:48.940
It’ll make me tear up and it’s just some
thing that they barely allude to, you know.

01:08:48.940 --> 01:08:55.339
Like a client a while back told me that—we
had had a session, a family session, where

01:08:55.339 --> 01:09:01.539
I had suggested to some of the members of
this person’s family that things might go

01:09:01.539 --> 01:09:10.549
better for them and their child, who was one
of my clients, if for the next two weeks what

01:09:10.549 --> 01:09:19.259
they practiced doing was watching each other’s
behaviour during the day and finding one thing

01:09:19.259 --> 01:09:21.429
that they could make a positive comment about.

01:09:21.429 --> 01:09:23.369
Now, and this isn’t a game.

01:09:23.369 --> 01:09:26.179
Like if you play it as a game then it isn’t
going to work.

01:09:26.179 --> 01:09:30.079
If you’re going to do this properly, what
you do is you watch and then you notice when

01:09:30.079 --> 01:09:34.609
a person does something that actually makes
you feel positively towards them, and then

01:09:34.609 --> 01:09:36.600
you tell them, “You did this.”

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:38.089
It has to be specific.

01:09:38.089 --> 01:09:40.500
It can’t be at the end of the day: “Well,
you were pretty good today.”

01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:43.009
It’s like: “No, that’s not going to
work, man.”

01:09:43.009 --> 01:09:45.359
You have to notice something they actually
did.

01:09:45.359 --> 01:09:53.609
It’s usually something trivial like: “I
really appreciate the fact that you took your

01:09:53.609 --> 01:09:57.820
dirty dinner plate off the table and rinsed
it off and put it in the dishwasher.”

01:09:57.820 --> 01:10:00.730
It’s like, you think that’s so damn trivial
it’s hardly worth commenting on.

01:10:00.730 --> 01:10:04.409
It’s like, no no no, it’s not at all;
it’s exactly the specific thing that you

01:10:04.409 --> 01:10:07.530
comment on if it did in fact make you happy.

01:10:07.530 --> 01:10:11.599
You know, and you can think about that in
behavioural terms.

01:10:11.599 --> 01:10:17.901
You could say if you something to do something
that you like you should tell them when they

01:10:17.901 --> 01:10:20.940
do things that you like so that they what
you like.

01:10:20.940 --> 01:10:24.590
But, of course, that means that you’re putting
yourself on the line too, right, because if

01:10:24.590 --> 01:10:28.000
the person knows what you like then they can
withhold it from you so you’re not going

01:10:28.000 --> 01:10:29.320
to do that unless you trust them.

01:10:29.320 --> 01:10:30.429
You’re really not.

01:10:30.429 --> 01:10:35.469
So, you know, you might be in a relationship—you
might be in a marriage—where never once

01:10:35.469 --> 01:10:38.900
during the whole bloody marriage do you ever
tell the other person what actually makes

01:10:38.900 --> 01:10:39.900
you happy.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:42.579
It’s like, that’s stupid by the way.

01:10:42.579 --> 01:10:46.480
So, like if you want have a really miserable
time of it that’s an excellent way to start.

01:10:46.480 --> 01:10:50.270
You could also add to that the presumption
that if they really loved you they’d know

01:10:50.270 --> 01:10:59.610
what you liked, which is also—
Your partner’s no smarter than you, generally

01:10:59.610 --> 01:11:02.920
speaking, and you don’t have a clue what’s
going to make them happy so I don’t know

01:11:02.920 --> 01:11:06.179
why you’d expect them to—especially if
they’re male—why you’d expect them to

01:11:06.179 --> 01:11:09.090
return the favour.

01:11:09.090 --> 01:11:10.570
So then you teach them, you know.

01:11:10.570 --> 01:11:12.090
Like it’ll probably take four years.

01:11:12.090 --> 01:11:15.840
It’s like: “I like it when you do this,”
and then, of course, they won’t so you might

01:11:15.840 --> 01:11:17.489
have to mention it again in a month.

01:11:17.489 --> 01:11:21.239
You might think, “Well, it’s going to
take eighteen months to train this person,

01:11:21.239 --> 01:11:22.239
at least.”

01:11:22.239 --> 01:11:25.710
I mean they’re smart; they’re smarter
than a dog but not much smarter.

01:11:25.710 --> 01:11:30.010
So you have to use repetition and you have
to be judicious about.

01:11:30.010 --> 01:11:33.840
And if you do that, and the other person is
also doing that to you, and you both know

01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:37.809
that you’re doing that, and you’re both
trying to make things better, then you’re

01:11:37.809 --> 01:11:41.130
going to communicate about it and then with
any luck things will get better.

01:11:41.130 --> 01:11:46.840
But that means you actually have to want things
to get better, and that’s part of the problem,

01:11:46.840 --> 01:11:50.800
of course, because not everybody wants things
to get better and there’s a part of everyone

01:11:50.800 --> 01:11:54.900
that would be just as happy if they didn’t
get better.

01:11:54.900 --> 01:12:03.150
So anyways, Rogers’ idea is that if you
communicate properly with the person that

01:12:03.150 --> 01:12:08.610
you’re in the relationship with that you
can model proper being, in some sense, and

01:12:08.610 --> 01:12:10.570
that at least you can attempt to model.

01:12:10.570 --> 01:12:14.090
And then as you attempt it you’ll get better
and better at it and as you get better at

01:12:14.090 --> 01:12:15.760
it the other person will get better at it.

01:12:15.760 --> 01:12:16.829
And that works too.

01:12:16.829 --> 01:12:23.110
So I had a client who was a very anxious person
so when the person first came to see me they

01:12:23.110 --> 01:12:30.920
couldn’t even talk on the phone, and now
they’re acting publicly—they’re acting

01:12:30.920 --> 01:12:32.340
in public.

01:12:32.340 --> 01:12:37.909
And that’s been the consequence of about
seven years of, I would say, Rogerian conversations

01:12:37.909 --> 01:12:42.670
where almost all that I did was listen.

01:12:42.670 --> 01:12:48.309
Now, I don’t quite get the unconditional
positive regard thing, although I know why

01:12:48.309 --> 01:12:54.170
Rogers said it, and his idea that arbitrary
conditions of worth can make someone pathological

01:12:54.170 --> 01:12:56.199
are true.

01:12:56.199 --> 01:13:02.120
But I think a more nuanced sense of unconditional
positive regard is what happens as you watch

01:13:02.120 --> 01:13:06.130
the person and they tell you something, and
as they’re telling it to you you can see

01:13:06.130 --> 01:13:08.310
a little part of them sort of come alive.

01:13:08.310 --> 01:13:13.570
And that often happens when their face loses
a fair bit of its rigidity and you can see

01:13:13.570 --> 01:13:16.909
more of what they were like when they were
a child sort of peeping through.

01:13:16.909 --> 01:13:18.610
So their face sort of opens up.

01:13:18.610 --> 01:13:19.980
You can think, “Aha!

01:13:19.980 --> 01:13:21.420
That was really good for that person.

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:24.809
Whatever they’ve been telling me that they’ve
been doing, that was right on the right track,”

01:13:24.809 --> 01:13:29.050
and then you could say, “Look, I noticed
when you talked about this, you know, your

01:13:29.050 --> 01:13:34.010
face relaxed, you didn’t look as anxious,
you were smiling—and it was genuine smile—for

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:37.280
like a second or two there it looked like
you were doing well and you were happy.”

01:13:37.280 --> 01:13:42.360
It’s like: “Let’s do a bunch more of
that,” because you know if you see in somebody

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:48.670
that they’re capable of experiencing that,
then you could help them experience that more,

01:13:48.670 --> 01:13:52.430
and that’s the purpose of a long term relationship.

01:13:52.430 --> 01:14:00.420
It’s that you have your weaknesses and inadequacies
and you can’t think yourself out of them

01:14:00.420 --> 01:14:04.400
by yourself, partly because they color the
way that you think, and so you need somebody

01:14:04.400 --> 01:14:09.730
else to bang yourself up against—to contend
with—so that all the knots in your soul

01:14:09.730 --> 01:14:10.920
can get ironed out.

01:14:10.920 --> 01:14:14.340
And hypothetically you’re doing the same
thing for the other person and then both of

01:14:14.340 --> 01:14:20.159
you are on an upwards spiral, and, in some
sense, that’s the core theory of Rogerian

01:14:20.159 --> 01:14:21.159
psychotherapy.

01:14:21.159 --> 01:14:29.960
It’s like: Genuine, honest communication
can heal people, and genuine dishonest communication

01:14:29.960 --> 01:14:31.660
can make them pathological.

01:14:31.660 --> 01:14:40.889
And in some sense I think Rogers got closer
to the truth—if you inferred Rogers’ presupposition,

01:14:40.889 --> 01:14:45.989
that’s as close to the truth as any psychotherapist
has got.

01:14:45.989 --> 01:14:50.570
Deceit makes people pathological, and that’s
true.

01:14:50.570 --> 01:14:54.190
A lot of—and Jung noticed this as well—a
lot of what you’re doing in psychotherapy

01:14:54.190 --> 01:14:57.100
and in any genuine relationship is a moral
endeavour.

01:14:57.100 --> 01:15:03.889
You’re trying to make things better, and
if the other person is deceiving themselves,

01:15:03.889 --> 01:15:08.119
or lying, or failing to become aware of things
that they should become aware of, they’re

01:15:08.119 --> 01:15:12.559
going to get bent and twisted out of shape
and then they’re not going to be congruent

01:15:12.559 --> 01:15:15.050
with their body or the broader world.

01:15:15.050 --> 01:15:18.699
And then they’re going to suffer, and then
that will cascade then all sorts of things

01:15:18.699 --> 01:15:19.800
will start to happen.

01:15:19.800 --> 01:15:25.110
And, you know, that’s the path to perdition
and an involuntary trip to the underworld.

01:15:25.110 --> 01:15:31.420
When we meet again I think I’ll show you
a little clip of Rogerian psychotherapy because

01:15:31.420 --> 01:15:36.219
a woman in the 1950’s agreed to be filmed,
which had a dramatic effect on her life by

01:15:36.219 --> 01:15:37.239
the way.

01:15:37.239 --> 01:15:45.260
But it provides a nice real world illustration
of what Rogers is doing when he’s trying

01:15:45.260 --> 01:15:45.780
to listen to people.

